deepDive:
Read the regime as four layers, each with less legal force than the one before. The first is Cap. 155 itself: the perimeter (ss.11–12), authorization under s.16 against the Seventh Schedule minimum criteria, supervision (on-site examination under s.55, returns under s.63), remedial and enforcement powers, approval of controllers and senior management, and official secrecy (s.120). The second is rules made by the MA: the Banking (Capital) Rules (Cap. 155L), Banking (Disclosure) Rules (Cap. 155M), Banking (Liquidity) Rules (Cap. 155Q) and Banking (Exposure Limits) Rules (Cap. 155S). These are law and hold most of the prudential figures your finance team reports against.

The third is HKMA soft law. Section 7(3) lets the MA publish guidelines on how he will exercise his functions. Section 16(10) supports the Guideline on Minimum Criteria for Authorization, which explains how he reads the Seventh Schedule. Guidelines create no offences, but they bite through the authorization criteria: the Seventh Schedule requires prudent conduct and adequate systems of control, and the MA measures both against SPM modules and guidelines. A sustained shortfall invites remedial action and conditions, and ultimately puts the licence at risk. Each SPM module states whether it is a [[statutory guideline]]. Module codes are a subject prefix plus a number: CG governance, TM technology, OR operational risk, SA supervisory approach, CA capital. Circulars and FAQs carry deadlines and the HKMA's current interpretation.

The fourth is the Code of Banking Practice; the revised Code took effect on 7 December 2023. It is non-statutory, but the Minimum Criteria Guideline expects compliance, the HKMA monitors it in regular supervision, and non-adherence can prompt a review of whether the bank still meets the criteria.

Other regulators, by activity:
- securities business: the bank is a [[registered institution]] under SFO s.119, supervised front-line by the HKMA applying SFC standards;
- insurance intermediary work: the Insurance Authority;
- personal data: the PCPD;
- deposit protection: the Deposit Protection Board (HK$800,000 cover since 1 October 2024);
- complaints: the FDRC scheme, which authorized institutions must join;
- suspicious transaction reports: the JFIU.

The MA is also the resolution authority under Cap. 628 and the AML relevant authority under Cap. 615.

How to research: start with HK e-Legislation, then the [[BRDR]] for guidelines, SPM modules, circulars and the Guide to Authorization.

provisions.summary:
Sets the minimum criteria an AI must meet and keep meeting. They cover:
- adequate home supervision of overseas applicants;
- fit and proper controllers, directors and chief executives;
- minimum capital and adequate financial resources and liquidity;
- adequate accounting and control systems;
- business conducted with integrity, prudence and professional competence.

Explains how the MA applies the Seventh Schedule to banks that deliver services mainly or only through electronic channels. It covers:
- local incorporation;
- ownership by a suitable parent, or through a Hong Kong intermediate holding company;
- physical presence;
- an exit plan.
Digital banks are supervised to the same standards as conventional banks, applied in a risk-based, technology-neutral way.
